WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.689
Solo: A Star Wars Story flew a little low
at the weekend box office, bringing in just

00:00:05.689 --> 00:00:10.900
$103 million over the four-day Memorial Day
weekend: a huge drop from early tracking,

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.700
which predicted the movie bringing in $170
million or more.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.760
It also marks the lowest opening for any one
of Disney's Star Wars films, below even Rogue

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.540
One, the franchise's first standalone film.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.380
Telling the origin story of the title smuggler,
Solo should have been able to capitalize on

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.360
the legacy of its hero to become a huge box
office draw.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.440
But there are a lot of factors that contributed
to Solo's disappointing box office performance,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.820
so let's take a look at the real reasons the
film didn't perform as well as expected.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:40.360
Star Wars fatigue

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:46.030
Solo is the fourth film to come out of the
new phase of the Star Wars franchise, and

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.820
the first not to hit theaters over the December
holidays.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.040
While this may have led to a diminished opening
weekend, it also meant that the movie hit

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:57.920
theaters just five months after the release
of The Last Jedi, marking the shortest window

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.380
between the release of two Star Wars films
to date.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.060
This may have contributed to some franchise
fatigue.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.680
While the trilogy films have build-up to bring
fans in no matter when they come out, the

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.740
standalone films have to find a way to make
themselves seem like must-see entertainment.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:16.840
Rogue One came out a full year after the last
Star Wars film, but for Solo, it may have

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.040
been hard to drum up interest so close to
the prior film's release.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:21.740
A new take

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:27.860
Alden Ehrenreich landed the difficult task
of trying to live up to Harrison Ford's iconic

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:32.260
portrayal of Han Solo, and although some people
have praised his performance, others don't

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:34.440
think he quite lives up to his predecessor.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.979
Part of the problem could be the script, which
shows off a much more optimistic version of

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:42.600
Han than the one audiences came to know and
love in the original trilogy.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.220
In the role, Ehrenreich came across as charming
and fun, but also different from Ford's version

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:48.880
of the character.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.440
While it was probably smart for the actor
not to try to do a direct impression, it still

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:57.720
might give some fans pause to see Han before
the weight of the galaxy got him down.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:01:59.740
Production drama

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:05.640
Good press is key to a movie's success, and
Solo was plagued by negative rumors after

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:10.289
a rocky production, which included the firing
of directors Phil Lord and Christopher Miller.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:14.660
Lord and Miller were said to have encouraged
more improvisation and a different tone than

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.320
the company wanted for the movie, so they
were replaced by Ron Howard.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.740
Even though Howard is a talented replacement,
the behind-the-scenes turmoil associated with

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.020
the picture may have left a lot of fans skeptical
about the finished product.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:31.200
And while reviews for the film were mostly
positive, they weren't as overwhelmingly favorable

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.400
as its predecessors.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.880
Even some critics who said the film was entertaining
also said it covered old ground and didn't

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.080
bring anything new or different to the franchise.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:44.480
Since word of mouth is essential for any movie
to succeed, Solo's ground campaign might've

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.580
been damaged by the initial bad buzz and ho-hum
critical reception.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:50.700
Holiday haze

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:56.560
Memorial Day weekend used to be the reliable
start of the summer box office, but its grosses

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.000
have been in a downward spiral in recent years.

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:05.340
The last time the weekend offered up a $100
million-plus opener was in 2014, with X-Men:

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.440
Days of Future Past.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:11.720
More recent offerings like Pirates of the
Caribbean: Dead Men Tell No Tales, Baywatch,

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:16.140
X-Men: Apocalypse, and Tomorrowland disappointed
in the same release time period.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:21.239
While big movies like Jurassic World: Fallen
Kingdom and The Incredibles 2 are likely to

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:25.959
save this summer from being a complete disaster,
Solo was stuck in a period that's been growing

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:30.400
consistently worse in recent years, making
it the summer's first big flop.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:32.720
Lingering competition

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:38.160
Most studios won't dare to challenge a Star
Wars movie, which is why it was the only major

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.620
release during its opening weekend.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:44.420
However, the film still faced competition
from the second weekend of Deadpool 2 and

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.300
the fifth weekend of Marvel's Avengers: Infinity
War.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.180
"He's trying to copy me."

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:54.560
Deadpool 2 was initially set to come out the
weekend after Solo, but Fox later moved the

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.340
release so that it hit theaters earlier.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:00.600
And even though Infinity War has been out
for weeks, it's still a box office draw.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.000
Solo might've managed to earn the top spot
at the box office on opening weekend, but

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:09.260
the ticket booth was still crowded with other
options that might've eaten away at its profits.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.240
International struggles

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:16.680
While overseas markets have been known to
save some movies from obscurity, Solo doesn't

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:18.940
seem to be resonating with international audiences.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:25.240
The film took in just $68 million in the international
markets after releasing in all major territories

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.020
except Japan.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:30.760
With an already large budget pushed higher
by the movie's reshoots, international profits

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:34.740
were important for Solo, but it doesn't look
like the film will be able to turn there for

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.140
relief if it continues to flop domestically.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.960
What does the movie's lackluster performance
mean for future Star Wars spinoffs?

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.440
"I've got a bad feeling about this."

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.180
Thanks for watching!

